    "content": " The Orion capsule is NASA’s crewed spacecraft for the Artemis program, designed to carry four astronauts to the Moon and into deep space, serving as a critical vehicle for future Mars missions. It features advanced life support, a 5,000°F heat shield, and is launched by the Space Launch System (SLS) rocket. This video shows the Artemis II crew as they prepare to board the Orion capsule:33sCBS NewsYouTube• Apr 1, 2026 33s Key Features and Functions: Mission Goal: Designed for long-duration deep space missions, enabling lunar exploration and supporting future human missions to Mars. Capacity & Size: Features a 330 cubic foot crew module, providing 57% more space than Apollo for four crew members for up to 21 days. Components: Consists of three primary elements: the Launch Abort System (LAS), the Crew Module (CM), and the European-built Service Module (ESM). Living Conditions: Includes sleeping bags, a galley, and a, \"hygiene bay\" (toilet). Safety & Return: Designed for high-speed re-entry (≈24,700 mph) from the Moon, with a heat shield protecting the crew. Recovery: Water landing is the primary method of recovery. Orion is built with an aluminum-lithium alloy and acts as the primary habitat for crews during transit in the Artemis lunar missions.",
